# Security checklist

Apply when writing or reviewing code that touches input, auth, data, network, files, or config.

## Secrets
- Secrets come from environment variables or a secret manager. Document names in `.env.example` with empty/fake values only.
- Never put real values in code, tests, fixtures, comments, logs, error messages, or CI files.
- If a secret was exposed, tell the user to rotate it. Deleting the commit is not enough.

```
// BAD
const apiKey = "sk-live-abc123";

// GOOD
const apiKey = process.env.API_KEY;
if (!apiKey) throw new Error("API_KEY is not set");
```

## Input and data
- Validate type, length, and range at every trust boundary (HTTP, CLI, file, env, DB).
- Use parameterized queries only. Never build SQL, shell, or paths by string concatenation.
- Avoid `eval`, `exec`, `shell=True`, and unsafe deserialization on untrusted data.
- Prevent path traversal: resolve and check paths against an allowed base directory.
- Do not log personal data (names, emails, job/company details) or full request bodies.

## Auth and network
- Enforce authorization on the server for every protected action. Never trust client-side checks.
- Use HTTPS, secure/HttpOnly/SameSite cookies, and an explicit CORS allowlist (no `*` with credentials).
- Hash passwords with a standard algorithm (argon2/bcrypt/scrypt). Never invent crypto.
- Error responses must not leak stack traces or internals.

## Dependencies
- Prefer maintained, widely used packages. Pin versions and commit the lockfile.
- Run the ecosystem audit (`npm audit`, `pip-audit`, `cargo audit`, etc.) before release.
